[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Relative Locators in Selenium"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selenium 4 introduced Relative Locators, formerly known as Friendly Locators, allowing automation engineers to locate elements based on their spatial relation to other elements on the web page. This guide covers how to use above(), below(), toLeftOf(), toRightOf(), and near()."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use relative locators, you must ensure you are importing the static relative locator methods correctly. Typically, you import them from the org.openqa.selenium.support.locators.RelativeLocator class."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import static org.openqa.selenium.support.locators.RelativeLocator.with;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Finding Elements Above and Below"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The above() and below() methods help you locate elements vertically relative to a known reference element. This is extremely useful for forms where labels are stacked directly above or below input fields."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
driver.get("https://example.com/login");

WebElement passwordLabel = driver.findElement(By.id("password-label"));
WebElement passwordInput = driver.findElement(with(By.tagName("input")).below(passwordLabel));

passwordInput.sendKeys("SecurePassword123");"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Finding Elements toLeftOf and toRightOf"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"When elements are aligned horizontally, such as table columns, button toolbars, or inline form fields, you can use toLeftOf() and toRightOf() to target the desired element."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"WebElement submitButton = driver.findElement(By.id("submit-btn"));
WebElement cancelButton = driver.findElement(with(By.tagName("button")).toLeftOf(submitButton));

cancelButton.click();"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Finding Near Elements"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The near() method locates an element that is close to the specified reference element. By default, it looks for elements within a 50-pixel radius, but you can explicitly define a pixel distance constraint."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"WebElement usernameField = driver.findElement(By.id("username"));
WebElement helperText = driver.findElement(with(By.tagName("span")).near(usernameField));

System.out.println(helperText.getText());"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Chaining Relative Locators"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can chain multiple relative locator conditions together to pinpoint an element that sits precisely at the intersection of spatial rules. For instance, finding an element that is both below a header and to the right of a sidebar."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"WebElement header = driver.findElement(By.id("main-header"));
WebElement sidebar = driver.findElement(By.id("left-sidebar"));

WebElement targetCell = driver.findElement(
    with(By.tagName("td"))
    .below(header)
    .toRightOf(sidebar)
);

targetCell.click();"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use a robust reference element that is unlikely to shift dynamically."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Combine relative locators with standard By locators (like tag name or attribute) to narrow down results quickly."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use chaining when a single directional method returns multiple ambiguous elements."}]
